
[[random-access-and-streaming]]
=== Random access and streaming

A stream is a list kept in a file instead of in memory. Use one when the data
is too large to hold at once, or when it is produced or consumed a batch at a
time. Three types give you a handle on such a file:

[cols="1,3", options="header"]
|===
| Type | Use

| `IFile [a]` | Read a finished file at random, by index or pattern.
| `IStream a` | Read a file forward, one batch of `a` at a time.
| `OStream a` | Write a file, one batch of `a` at a time.
|===

A handle is an ordinary Morloc value. You can pass it to another function, put
it in a list, or hand it to code that runs in another language; the receiver
reads the same file. All three are opened with `@open`, which returns a `Try`
(`Err` if the file is missing or unreadable), and released with `@close`. The
`::` annotation on `@open` says which kind of handle you want and what the
elements are.

The examples below are one file:

[source, morloc]
.people.loc
----
module people (writePeople, lookup, slice, count, batches, walk)

import root-py

record Person = Person { name :: Str, age :: Int }

writePeople :: Str -> <IO> ()
writePeople path = do
  Ok o <- @open path :: <IO> (Try Str (OStream Person))
  @write 3 o [{name = "Alice", age = 30}, {name = "Bob", age = 25}]
  @flush o
  @write 3 o [{name = "Carol", age = 41}]
  @close o

lookup :: Str -> Int -> <IO> Person
lookup path i = do
  Ok f <- @open path :: <IO> (Try Str (IFile [Person]))
  let p = .[i] f
  @close f
  p

slice :: Str -> <IO> [Str]
slice path = do
  Ok f <- @open path :: <IO> (Try Str (IFile [Person]))
  .[1:3].name f

count :: Str -> <IO> Int
count path = do
  Ok f <- @open path :: <IO> (Try Str (IFile [Person]))
  Ok n <- @flen f
  n

batches :: Str -> <IO> [[Person]]
batches path = do
  Ok s <- @open path :: <IO> (Try Str (IStream Person))
  Ok a <- @next s
  Ok b <- @next s
  Ok c <- @next s
  @close s
  [a, b, c]

walk :: Str -> <IO> ([Person], Person)
walk path = do
  Ok f <- @open path :: <IO> (Try Str (IFile [Person]))
  s <- @stream f
  Ok a <- @next s
  (a, .[2] f)
----

[WARNING]
.Element types are not checked yet
====

A stream file records the type of its elements, and opening it at a different
type should be an `Err`. Today only some opens check it; an `IFile` open never
does, so reading a file of `Int` as `IFile [Real]` returns garbage instead of
failing. Open a file at the type it was written with.

====

==== Writing with OStream

`@open` on an `OStream` creates the file, replacing any file already there.
`@write level o xs` appends the list `xs`. `level` is a compression level from
0 (none) to 9 (see <<compression>>); every write to one stream must use the
same level. `@close` finishes the file, after which it can be read.

[source, console]
----
$ ./people writePeople people.dat
----

Writes are buffered, and each full buffer becomes one batch in the file.
`@flush o` ends the current batch early. Without the `@flush` above, all three
people would land in one batch.

`@append path` opens a stream file for further writes, creating it if it is
not there. `@concat paths dest` joins compatible stream files into `dest`;
`dest` may be one of `paths`, so adding a batch file to a log is
`@concat [log, batch] log`. A failed `@concat` leaves `dest` as it was.

==== Random access with IFile

An `IFile [a]` reads elements without loading the file. It takes the same
patterns as an in-memory list (<<patterns>>), and `@flen` returns the element
count from the file's footer without reading the data:

[source, console]
----
$ ./people lookup people.dat 1
{"name":"Bob","age":25}
$ ./people slice people.dat
["Bob","Carol"]
$ ./people count people.dat
3
----

==== Sequential reading with IStream

`@next s` returns the next batch as a list, and `Ok []` once the file is
exhausted. Batches come back as they were written:

[source, console]
----
$ ./people batches people.dat
[[{"name":"Alice","age":30},{"name":"Bob","age":25}],[{"name":"Carol","age":41}],[]]
----

`@stream f` turns an open `IFile [a]` into an `IStream a` with its own cursor,
so walking it does not disturb indexed reads of `f`. (The CLI chapter also has
an `@stream`, a modifier on output actions (<<streaming-output>>); it is
unrelated.)

[source, console]
----
$ ./people walk people.dat
[[{"name":"Alice","age":30},{"name":"Bob","age":25}],{"name":"Carol","age":41}]
----

==== Standard input and output

`@stdin` opens standard input as an `IStream`, and `@stderr` opens standard
error as an `OStream` handle; both take their element type from an
annotation, like `@open`, and use the same `@next`, `@write` and `@close`.
`@stdout` hands a body the standard output stream, and the command returns
`Collect a`, so its signature says what it writes:

[source, morloc]
----
@stdout :: (OStream a -> <IO, e> b) -> <IO, e> (Collect a)
----

`@stdout` closes the handle when the body returns. Like `@collect`, it ends a
command whose signature returns `Collect` (<<streaming-output>>). This lets one
Morloc program pipe typed data into another:

[source, morloc]
.pipe.loc
----
module pipe (produce, double)

import root-py

produce :: <IO> (Collect Int)
produce = @stdout (\o -> do
  _ <- @write 0 o [1, 2, 3]
  @write 0 o [4, 5, 6])

double :: <IO> (Collect Int)
double = @stdout (\o -> do
  Ok s <- @stdin :: <IO> (Try Str (IStream Int))
  Ok xs <- @next s
  _ <- @write 0 o (map (\x -> x * 2) xs)
  Ok ys <- @next s
  _ <- @write 0 o (map (\x -> x * 2) ys)
  @close s)
----

[source, console]
----
$ ./pipe -f packet produce | ./pipe double
[2,4,6,8,10,12]
----

By default a program prints its output as JSON for people to read. `-f packet`
asks the writing end for Morloc's binary format instead, which carries the
element type so the reader can check it. The last program in the pipeline
prints JSON as usual. <<reading-stdin>> covers the command-line side: reading
a file argument or standard input with the same code, and what standard input
may carry.

[WARNING]
.Foreign code must not write to an open standard stream
====

If a sourced function prints to standard output (`print` in Python,
`std::cout` in {cpp}, `cat` in R) while Morloc has it open as an `OStream`,
the text lands among the binary batches and the reader fails with
`invalid magic`. Send diagnostics to standard error, or write the stream to a
file instead.

====

.How stream files are stored
[%collapsible]
====

This is for readers who want the mechanism; the full specification, including
the file layout and how several processes share one open stream, is
<<build-streaming>>.

Each batch is compressed on its own, so any batch can be read without the
others. An `IFile` keeps recently decompressed batches in a per-handle cache,
so nearby reads do not decompress again; a slice that spans several batches
decompresses them in parallel. An element larger than the write buffer is
written as a batch of its own rather than split.

`@concat` copies the batches byte for byte, with no decoding. It builds the
result beside `dest` and renames it into place, which is why `dest` may also be
a source and why a failure leaves the old `dest` intact.

====

==== A stream its writer did not finish

A stream file ends with a footer that records how its writer stopped. A
reader refuses a stream whose writer failed rather than hand over part of it
as if it were whole: `@load`, an `IStream`, an `IFile`, `@concat` and
`@append` all do. A stream read from a pipe also fails when the input ends
before its final footer. A run that fails after it began streaming packet
output ends that output with a footer that says it failed. A file with no
footer may still be being written, so an `IStream` reads the batches it
holds; an `IFile` needs a final footer and refuses it.

`morloc-nexus file` shows how a stream ended, and `--validate` fails on one
that did not finish. To read what such a file holds, repair it: the repair
keeps every complete batch and marks the file repaired, which readers accept.

[source, console]
----
$ morloc-nexus file cut.pkt
cut.pkt: stream-packet schema="aj" state=missing subpackets=2 elements=3 ...
$ morloc-nexus file --repair cut.pkt
cut.pkt: repaired: 2 sub-packets, 3 elements
----

A repaired file may hold fewer batches than its writer meant to write.
`@concat` keeps the mark on any file it builds from one, and `@append` will
not extend it.

[[stream-env-vars]]
==== Environment variables

[cols="1,3", options="header"]
|===
| Variable | Effect

| `MORLOC_REGISTRY_SLOT_COUNT`
| How many stream handles one run may hold open at once. Default `4096`.

| `MORLOC_WRITE_BUFFER_BYTES`
| Size of an `OStream` write buffer, and so of a batch. Default 16 MiB.
  Smaller gives finer-grained batches at more overhead per batch.

| `MORLOC_IFILE_CACHE_BYTES`
| Size of each `IFile` handle's cache of decompressed batches. Default
  256 MiB; `0` disables it.
|===

`MORLOC_FRAME_WORKERS`, the number of threads that compress and decompress
large batches, applies here too (see <<compression>>).

